WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.029
Some people learn the hard way – don't threaten
Batman with a good time.

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:08.610
Comic book aficionados know all too well to
keep their eyes extra peeled during a new

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.780
superhero movie.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:14.519
From Marvel to DC, these films often contain
an assortment of Easter eggs and hidden references

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:18.599
designed to reward those who have kept up
with the characters' comic book roots.

00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:22.390
Sometimes these references are thrown in just
for fun, while in other cases, they're designed

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.300
to hint at greater machinations at play.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.359
"The Batman" certainly has no shortage of
Easter eggs.

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:31.230
Right from the very beginning, the very first
murder takes place on Halloween night.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.380
It's hard to hear that without immediately
thinking of one of the best, most popular

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.260
Batman stories to ever grace the pages of
a comic book — "The Long Halloween."

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.510
The rest of the film may deviate from that
storyline, but the influences of that story

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.850
remain clear.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.960
There's also the quick line Catwoman gives
toward the end of the film where she mentions

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.960
heading to Blüdhaven, which has great significance
to the lore of DC Comics.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.230
Of course, not every Easter egg has to deal
with Batman himself.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:58.300
We found one that seems like it's referencing
one of star Robert Pattinson's previous cinematic

00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.460
outings.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.510
Robert Pattinson has an extensive filmography.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.339
While most general moviegoers may know him
for his role of Edward in the "Twilight" series,

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:09.710
he has a litany of independent projects that
prove he's a master at his craft.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:13.570
Anyone who has seen him perform in "Cosmopolis,"
"High Life," or "The Lighthouse" was surely

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.299
ecstatic when the announcement came that he
would soon take on the role of Bruce Wayne.

00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:21.369
However, there's a little-known movie Pattinson
starred in that's certainly worth your time,

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:24.749
especially seeing how it may have received
a shoutout in "The Batman."

00:01:24.749 --> 00:01:29.450
2017's "Good Time" sees Pattinson play Connie
Nikas, a criminal on the run after a robbery

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:30.450
goes sideways.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.729
It's an intense thriller from the Safdie Brothers,
who would go on to give the world a more high-profile

00:01:34.729 --> 00:01:37.679
thriller with the Adam Sandler-starring "Uncut
Gems."

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:40.960
The film earned rave reviews from critics
and general audiences, according to Rotten

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.900
Tomatoes, so you should check it out if you
have the chance.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.130
You can rent or purchase it on Amazon Prime
Video if you're interested in checking out

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.740
a great non-superhero movie that will still
get your blood pumping.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.679
The movie also seemed to make an appearance
in "The Batman."

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.429
Early in the film, there's an establishing
shot of a market with an awning reading "Good

00:01:56.429 --> 00:01:57.429
Times Grocery."

00:01:57.429 --> 00:02:00.530
It's hard not to see that and not immediately
think of Pattinson's resume.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:04.500
Of course, it could just be a coincidence,
but it's fun to think Matt Reeves wanted to

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.429
include a lesser-known Pattinson Easter egg
in his superhero flick.

00:02:07.429 --> 00:02:11.330
Plenty of people may wonder why director Matt
Reeves would specifically want to give a shoutout

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:15.050
to "Good Time" as opposed to any other movie
in Pattinson's filmography.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.760
As it turns out, "Good Time" played a significant
role in getting Pattinson the part of Bruce

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.480
Wayne in the first place.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.790
In an interview with Esquire, Reeves mentioned
watching "Good Time" and immediately knew

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.130
Pattinson was his guy.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:28.090
He elaborated,
"In the process of writing ["The Batman"],

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:31.790
I watched "Good Time," and I thought, 'Okay,
he's got an inner kind of rage that connects

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:35.660
with this character and a dangerousness, and
I can feel this desperation.'

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.760
And I became dead-set on it being Rob."

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.200
Reeves goes on to mention how he didn't even
know at the time if Pattinson would have any

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.380
interest in taking on the character, but he
so desperately wanted him to be his Bruce

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:46.380
Wayne.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.840
As it turns out, Pattinson was more than game,
provided he could do a really dirty, slimy

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.310
version of the character.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:55.670
As Pattinson stated in an interview with MovieMaker,
"At the time, even my agents thought it pretty

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.610
out of character to just suddenly get fixated
on Batman.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.200
And I didn't even really know the status of
the project."

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.420
Both men got their wishes as Reeves was able
to cast Pattinson, and the actor indeed plays

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.260
a dirtier iteration of the Caped Crusader
in the film.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.500
He went on to tell Premiere,
"[Batman] lives a criminal life, but without

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:12.570
committing crimes!

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.640
I felt like I could get something out of that.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.370
Anyway, I could only play a superhero if he
was really dirty!"

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:22.360
Everything worked out for the best in the
end; turns out Pattinson had a good time as

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.700
Batman after all.

